Here's a complete solution for your wind flow rate meter using ESP8266 (NodeMCU), JHD16A LCD display, and a PC cooling fan as an anemometer. The system displays wind speed on both the LCD and a mobile phone via a local web server.

## 🔌 Wiring Diagram

### PC Fan as Wind Sensor
```
PC Fan Red Wire (+)  ---->| [1N4148 Diode] ----> [10kΩ Resistor] ----> NodeMCU A0
                                                    |
                                                 [0.1µF Capacitor]
                                                    |
PC Fan Black Wire (-)  ---------------------------> GND
```
*Note: This half-wave rectifier + low-pass filter converts the fan's AC voltage to smoothed DC readable by the ADC. For better accuracy, use a full-wave bridge rectifier.*

### JHD16A LCD (I2C Backpack)
```
LCD VCC  --------------> 5V (or 3.3V if your module supports it)
LCD GND  --------------> GND
LCD SDA  --------------> NodeMCU D2 (GPIO4)
LCD SCL  --------------> NodeMCU D1 (GPIO5)
```

### NodeMCU Power
- Connect via USB or external 5V/Vin pin
- Ensure common ground between fan circuit, LCD, and NodeMCU

## 📋 Required Libraries
Install via Arduino IDE Library Manager:
1. `ESP8266WiFi` (built-in)
2. `ESP8266WebServer` (built-in)
3. `LiquidCrystal_I2C` by Frank de Brabander

## 💻 Complete Code

```cpp
#include <ESP8266WiFi.h>
#include <ESP8266WebServer.h>
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>
#include <Wire.h>

// ==================== USER CONFIGURATION ====================
const char* WIFI_SSID = "YOUR_WIFI_SSID";
const char* WIFI_PASSWORD = "YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD";

// Calibration factor: adjust based on your fan's characteristics
// Formula: Wind Speed (m/s) = Voltage (V) * CALIBRATION_FACTOR
// Start with 1.0 and adjust via testing against a known wind source
const float CALIBRATION_FACTOR = 2.0;  // Example: 1V = 2 m/s

// LCD configuration (most common I2C address is 0x27)
LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2);
// If 0x27 doesn't work, try 0x3F

// Web server on port 80
ESP8264WebServer server(80);

// Volatile variable for wind speed (updated in loop, read by server)
volatile float currentWindSpeed = 0.0;

// ==================== SETUP ====================
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Initialize LCD
  lcd.init();
  lcd.backlight();
  lcd.clear();
  lcd.print("Wind Meter");
  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print("Initializing...");

  // Connect to WiFi
  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);
  Serial.print("Connecting to WiFi");
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {
    delay(500);
    Serial.print(".");
  }
  Serial.println("\nConnected!");
  Serial.print("IP Address: ");
  Serial.println(WiFi.localIP());

  lcd.clear();
  lcd.print("WiFi Connected");
  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print(WiFi.localIP().toString().c_str());
  delay(2000);

  // Setup web server routes
  server.on("/", handleRoot);
  server.on("/data", handleData);  // JSON endpoint for AJAX
  server.begin();
  Serial.println("HTTP server started");

  lcd.clear();
}

// ==================== MAIN LOOP ====================
void loop() {
  // Read analog value from A0 (0-1023)
  int sensorValue = analogRead(A0);

  // Convert to voltage (0-3.3V)
  float voltage = sensorValue * (3.3 / 1023.0);

  // Convert to wind speed (m/s)
  // Apply calibration factor - adjust this based on your fan's output
  float windSpeed = voltage * CALIBRATION_FACTOR;

  // Optional: smoothing with moving average (uncomment if needed)
  // static float readings[10] = {0};
  // static uint8_t index = 0;
  // float sum = 0;
  // readings[index] = windSpeed;
  // index = (index + 1) % 10;
  // for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) sum += readings[i];
  // windSpeed = sum / 10;

  currentWindSpeed = windSpeed;

  // Update LCD display
  lcd.clear();
  lcd.print("Wind Speed:");
  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print(windSpeed, 1);  // Show 1 decimal place
  lcd.print(" m/s");

  // Handle web clients
  server.handleClient();

  // Small delay for stability
  delay(200);
}

// ==================== WEB SERVER HANDLERS ====================
void handleRoot() {
  String html = R"=====(
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Wind Flow Meter</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <style>
        body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; text-align: center; margin-top: 50px; }
        .container { max-width: 400px; margin: 0 auto; }
        .wind-speed { font-size: 48px; font-weight: bold; color: #2c3e50; margin: 20px 0; }
        .unit { font-size: 24px; color: #7f8c8d; }
        .status { padding: 10px; background: #ecf0f1; border-radius: 5px; margin: 20px 0; }
        .refresh { 
            background: #3498db; 
            color: white; 
            border: none; 
            padding: 10px 20px; 
            border-radius: 5px; 
            cursor: pointer;
            font-size: 16px;
        }
        .refresh:hover { background: #2980b9; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Wind Flow Meter</h1>
        <div class="status">Connected to ESP8266</div>
        <div class="wind-speed" id="windSpeed">--.--</div>
        <div class="unit">m/s</div>
        <button class="refresh" onclick="location.reload()">Refresh</button>
        <p>Last updated: <span id="timestamp">--:--:--</span></p>
    </div>
    <script>
        function updateDisplay() {
            fetch('/data')
                .then(response => response.json())
                .then(data => {
                    document.getElementById('windSpeed').textContent = data.speed.toFixed(1);
                    document.getElementById('timestamp').textContent = 
                        new Date().toLocaleTimeString();
                })
                .catch(err => {
                    document.getElementById('windSpeed').textContent = "ERR";
                });
        }

        // Update every second
        setInterval(updateDisplay, 1000);

        // Initial load
        updateDisplay();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
)=====";

  server.send(200, "text/html", html);
}

void handleData() {
  String json = "{\"speed\":";
  json += String(currentWindSpeed, 2);  // 2 decimal places for JSON
  json += "}";
  server.send(200, "application/json", json);
}
```

## 📝 How to Use

1. **Wire everything** according to the diagram above
2. **Upload the code** to your NodeMCU using Arduino IDE
3. **Configure WiFi**: Replace `YOUR_WIFI_SSID` and `YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD` with your network credentials
4. **Power on** the system
5. **Read the IP address** from the LCD (it shows after connecting to WiFi)
6. **Access from mobile**: Open a browser on your phone connected to the same WiFi and go to `http://[ESP_IP_ADDRESS]`

## 🔧 Calibration Guide

Since PC fans vary in output voltage, you'll need to calibrate:

1. **Test with known wind source** (use a fan with adjustable speed or anemometer if available)
2. **Measure voltage** at A0 using a multimeter at different wind speeds
3. **Adjust CALIBRATION_FACTOR** in the code:
   ```
   CALIBRATION_FACTOR = Known_Wind_Speed (m/s) / Measured_Voltage (V)
   ```
4. **Typical starting values**:
   - Small PC fan: Try 1.5 - 3.0
   - Larger fan: May need smaller factor (0.5 - 1.5)

## 📊 Features

- **LCD Display**: Shows real-time wind speed in m/s
- **Mobile Web Interface**: 
  - Real-time updates every second
  - Accessible from any device on your local network
  - Clean, responsive design
- **Wind Speed Calculation**: Converts analog voltage to meaningful units
- **Noise Reduction**: Built-in ADC reading with optional moving average (commented out)
- **Status Feedback**: LCD shows connection status during boot, web page shows connection status

## 🛠️ Troubleshooting

1. **LCD shows blank boxes**:
   - Check I2C wiring (SDA/D2, SCL/D1)
   - Try different I2C address (0x3F instead of 0x27)
   - Ensure LCD has proper power

2. **Wind speed always zero**:
   - Check fan wiring and diode orientation
   - Measure voltage at A0 with multimeter while blowing on fan
   - Verify analogRead is changing values

3. **Cannot connect to web server**:
   - Verify WiFi credentials
   - Check IP address on LCD
   - Ensure mobile is on same network
   - Try accessing via IP directly (not through search bar)

4. **Erratic readings**:
   - Add better smoothing (uncomment moving average code)
   - Check for electrical interference
   - Ensure proper grounding

## ⚠️ Safety Notes

- The PC fan generates voltage when spun - **do not connect to high-voltage sources**
- Keep fan away from liquids
- Use appropriate wire gauges for current
- The NodeMCU 3.3V ADC is tolerant - ensure input voltage never exceeds 3.3V (use voltage divider if needed)

This setup gives you both local LCD display and remote monitoring capability for your wind flow measurement project. Adjust the calibration factor to match your specific fan characteristics for accurate readings!
